      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "ximgproc edge-aware smoothing - structure/detail separation\n\nSix cv2.ximgproc smoothers on the same photo. They all remove detail while trying to KEEP edges, but they disagree about what an edge is, and that is the whole point of the sheet. 'CV Image Quality' scores each result against the original with SSIM, so you can read off how much structure each one preserved.\n\n  guidedFilter          a local linear fit to a GUIDE image (here the photo itself, i.e. self-guided). Fast, no gradient reversal.\n  l0Smooth              minimises the NUMBER of non-zero gradients, so it produces flat cartoon-like regions with very sharp steps.\n  rollingGuidanceFilter iteratively removes structures SMALLER than sigmaSpace while restoring the large edges - a scale-space filter.\n  fastGlobalSmootherFilter  a global weighted-least-squares fit; it considers the WHOLE image at once instead of a local window, so it is the one to use when a gradient must stay smooth across the frame.\n  edgePreservingFilter  a local histogram filter (ximgproc's, NOT the cv2.edgePreservingFilter in the photo module - two different functions that share a name, which is exactly why contrib nodes keep their module in the node id).\n  weightedMedianFilter  a joint-histogram median; the best of the set at removing salt-and-pepper without rounding corners.\n\nNOT IN THIS SHEET - ximgproc.anisotropicDiffusion. It is exposed as a node, but measurement says its K (the Perona-Malik edge threshold) is inert in this OpenCV build: sweeping K from 1 to 5000 moves the edge slope by a few percent, and steps of every height are smoothed alike, so it behaves as a plain isotropic blur. It also treats everything outside the image as BLACK, so a dark rim creeps inward about a pixel per three iterations, and alpha >= 0.2 makes it DIVERGE into 0/255 ringing. Its tooltips say all of this; use one of the six above instead.\n\nTHE eps TRAP: guidedFilter's eps is a variance, i.e. SQUARED units of the input's own scale. On ordinary uint8 (0-255) data eps <= 1.0 is an exact no-op and the useful range is roughly 100-600 - it is NOT the 0-1 knob the name suggests. The value here is 400 (about a 20-level tolerance). If you feed float 0-1 data instead, divide by 255^2."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
